{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017TzUjcRrypH9zHUfHh3BDd","type":"tool_result","content":"ideas/01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval/:\nactive_idea.json\nactive_rubrics.json\nagent_claude_20261111_141611.log\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nidea_bundle.json\nidea_generation_wrapper_output.log\nmemory.md\nprogram_0\nprogram_1\nprompt.md\nrubric_status.json\ntarget_rubrics.json\ntask.md\nwrapper_output.log\n\nideas/01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n{\n  \"combined_score\": -0.1479678140153992,\n  \"correct\": true,\n  \"entrypoint_path\": \"src/program.py\",\n  \"program_dir\": \"work/results/a06/claude/20261111_111240/investigation/ideas/01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval/program_1\",\n  \"source_attempt_id\": \"c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval_attempt_01\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-11-11T14:18:48.269590+00:00\"\n}{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"The interval score at level 0.90 is minimised by the 5% and 95% quantiles of the predictive distribution of the selected config's fresh-question accuracy y. The naive reporter (best search score + Wilson) is biased upward (winner's curse) and too narrow in the 'many configs, few questions' regime. Searcher-agnostic debiasing should lower the mean interval score: fit a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model P(correct)=sigmoid(a_k - b_q), with a_k ~ N(0,tau^2) and b_q ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), to ALL logged (question, config, outcome) triples with tau and s set by empirical Bayes. Because both exhaustive search and successive halving choose configs using only already-observed outcomes, selection is ignorable for the likelihood. The posterior of theta_k = E_b[sigmoid(a_k - b)] for the selected config is therefore valid under either searcher without a searcher-specific correction. Shrinkage toward the pooled mean removes the winner's-curse bias; with tau estimated near 0 it handles the all-configs-equal worlds. Posterior quantiles (Laplace draws of a_k, mu, s, then Gauss-Hermite integration over fresh difficulties) give the interval. Expected result: lower mean interval score than the naive Wilson baseline (combined_score -0.232), with the biggest gain at large K, small N and the halving searcher. Failure mode: real MMLU-Pro outcomes are multi-dimensional (subject x config interactions), so the 1-D model can be miscalibrated there. A held-out-question calibration widening factor and a bootstrap-style fallback are the safeguards.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"In program_1/src/program.py, keep the handle(request) python_rpc_v1 interface. reset stores the seed and returns exactly {'ok': True}. report returns exactly estimate, lower and upper, clipped so that 0<=lower<=estimate<=upper<=1 and all values are finite floats.\",\n    \"Decode log.question_idx, log.config_idx, log.outcome and subject_code from base64 little-endian arrays with np.frombuffer (dtypes uint16/uint8/uint8/uint8). Work only with arrays from the request, using no files or network.\",\n    \"Implement _fit_irt(q, c, y, subj, N, K): MAP fit of logit P = a[c] - b[q] by alternating damped Newton steps. Ridge priors a ~ N(0,tau^2), b ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), with a per-subject mean shrunk to the global mean. Update tau^2 and s^2 each outer iteration from the posterior second moments. Include the Laplace/trace correction so tau^2 does not collapse or inflate. Clamp tau^2 to [1e-4, 4].\",\n    \"Work only with sparse triples so the same code handles the exhaustive (N*K entries) and halving (sparse log) searchers. Use vectorised numpy with np.bincount for the per-config and per-question gradient and Hessian diagonals. Cap total outer iterations (about 30) and add a wall-clock guard (about 1.5 s) that returns the best current fit.\",\n    \"Posterior for the selected config: approximate Var(a_k) by the inverse of the diagonal Hessian plus the uncertainty in mu. Draw S (about 400) samples of (a_k, mu_global, log s) with the reset-seeded np.random.default_rng. For each draw compute theta = sum_j w_j * sigmoid(a_k - mu_sub_mix - s*x_j) using np.polynomial.hermite_e.hermegauss nodes, mixing over the empirical subject distribution when subject_code is not all zero.\",\n    \"Report estimate = posterior median or mean of theta, lower = np.quantile(theta, 0.05) and upper = np.quantile(theta, 0.95). Then apply a width-calibration factor kappa >= 1, applied around the estimate, that depends on min(N, evidence), K and whether subject_code is all zero (real vs synthetic).\",\n    \"Degenerate guards. If all outcomes are 0, return estimate 0, lower 0 and an upper bound no greater than a rule-of-three bound (about 3/n_selected_obs, capped, e.g. <=0.02). If the selected config has no observations, fall back to the pooled mean with a wide interval. If tau is estimated at its lower clamp (all configs equal), report a pooled-mean interval.\",\n    \"Build a local dev harness OUTSIDE program_1/src (for example dev/sim.py). It should generate synthetic latent-difficulty worlds across N in {50..2000} and K in {2..64}, plus equal-config worlds, and run both exhaustive and successive-halving searchers. It should also replay ../../task/resources/search/outcomes.npz (24 configs) with both searchers on subsampled question sets, compute the true y from held-out questions, and compute the mean interval score IS=(u-l)+20*max(l-y,0)+20*max(y-u,0). Compare the naive Wilson baseline, the posterior interval without calibration, and the calibrated version.\",\n    \"Use the dev harness to choose kappa and the clamps on the search split only. Check coverage near 0.90 in each (searcher, K, N) stratum. Check that the average per-call time is <=1 s and the worst-case is <=5 s on 4 threads. Set OMP/MKL threads to <=4. Optionally add a held-out-question calibration check inside report (a fit on 80% of questions, scoring the selected config on the other 20%) only if it improves IS in the dev harness.\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_agent\": \"Interface (from the baseline docstring in task/program_0/src/program.py): python_rpc_v1 handle(request). The ops are reset and report, with exact response key sets. numpy and scipy only, 4 threads, average <=2.7 s per call. The score is the interval score at 0.90, so the optimum is the 5%/95% predictive quantiles of y (the selected config's true fresh accuracy). A well-calibrated posterior interval beats a narrow Wilson interval because the 20x miss penalty is heavy at the winner's-curse bias. Configuration indices are relabelled at random per request, so use no index priors. Request mix is unlabelled: synthetic latent-difficulty worlds, equal-config worlds, all-zero worlds, and real MMLU-Pro (24 configs; exhaustive or successive halving). Use ../../task/resources/search (manifest.json, outcomes.npz) only to build the local dev harness. Do not read evaluate.py/evaluate_final.py to learn hidden details. Keep the implementation in the order of the plan. A secondary option, if the 1-D IRT is miscalibrated on real data: for the exhaustive searcher only, the question-bootstrap optimism estimate (resample questions, re-select the argmax, measure boot-selected in-bag mean minus its original-data mean) combined with the posterior interval, picking the wider of the two. Suggested code names for the rubric snippets: _fit_irt, tau2, kappa, hermegauss, np.quantile, time.perf_counter. If halving-round structure matters, it is inferable by grouping the log by question_idx (each question is evaluated by all survivors of its round). Since the likelihood is ignorable under adaptive selection, no round structure is needed for the base method. Limitations to record: 1-D latent difficulty vs real multi-dimensional subject x config effects; the estimated tau is noisy at small N.\",\n  \"target_rubrics\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"combined_score > -0.2320671337621132\",\n      \"id\": \"incumbent_score_improvement\",\n      \"metric\": \"combined_score\",\n      \"operator\": \">\",\n      \"threshold\": -0.2320671337621132,\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"program.py fits a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model a[config]-b[question] on the sparse (question, config, outcome) triples. It uses empirical-Bayes updates of the config-ability variance tau^2 and the difficulty variance s^2, via bincount-based vectorised Newton steps. The same code path serves both the exhaustive and the halving searchers, and the selected config's posterior is used for the report.\",\n      \"id\": \"hierarchical_irt_fit_on_sparse_log\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"def _fit_irt(\",\n          \"np.bincount(\",\n          \"tau2\",\n          \"hermegauss\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"The reported interval is the 5%/95% quantile of posterior draws of the selected config's fresh-question accuracy (Gauss-Hermite integration over fresh difficulties). A width-calibration factor kappa and ordering/clipping guarantees ensure 0<=lower<=estimate<=upper<=1, and the response has exactly the keys estimate, lower and upper.\",\n      \"id\": \"posterior_quantile_interval_with_calibration\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"np.quantile(\",\n          \"0.05\",\n          \"0.95\",\n          \"kappa\",\n          \"default_rng(\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"program.py handles all-zero outcomes (rule-of-three-style capped upper bound), equal-config worlds (tau at lower clamp gives a pooled-mean interval) and an unobserved selected config. It also enforces a per-call wall-clock guard well under 10 s via time.perf_counter.\",\n      \"id\": \"degenerate_world_and_time_guards\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"time.perf_counter(\",\n          \"def handle(\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"title\": \"Empirical-Bayes latent-difficulty posterior interval\"\n}\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace/paper","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d9f3fff-e236-4c90-ae14-7a47dddbd380","uuid":"1ec8630f-1672-418e-b3ab-ea01fcf84995","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:30:09.401Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ideas/01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval/:\nactive_idea.json\nactive_rubrics.json\nagent_claude_20261111_141611.log\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nidea_bundle.json\nidea_generation_wrapper_output.log\nmemory.md\nprogram_0\nprogram_1\nprompt.md\nrubric_status.json\ntarget_rubrics.json\ntask.md\nwrapper_output.log\n\nideas/01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n{\n  \"combined_score\": -0.1479678140153992,\n  \"correct\": true,\n  \"entrypoint_path\": \"src/program.py\",\n  \"program_dir\": \"work/results/a06/claude/20261111_111240/investigation/ideas/01_empirical_bayes_latent_difficulty_posterior_interval/program_1\",\n  \"source_attempt_id\": \"c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval_attempt_01\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-11-11T14:18:48.269590+00:00\"\n}{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"The interval score at level 0.90 is minimised by the 5% and 95% quantiles of the predictive distribution of the selected config's fresh-question accuracy y. The naive reporter (best search score + Wilson) is biased upward (winner's curse) and too narrow in the 'many configs, few questions' regime. Searcher-agnostic debiasing should lower the mean interval score: fit a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model P(correct)=sigmoid(a_k - b_q), with a_k ~ N(0,tau^2) and b_q ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), to ALL logged (question, config, outcome) triples with tau and s set by empirical Bayes. Because both exhaustive search and successive halving choose configs using only already-observed outcomes, selection is ignorable for the likelihood. The posterior of theta_k = E_b[sigmoid(a_k - b)] for the selected config is therefore valid under either searcher without a searcher-specific correction. Shrinkage toward the pooled mean removes the winner's-curse bias; with tau estimated near 0 it handles the all-configs-equal worlds. Posterior quantiles (Laplace draws of a_k, mu, s, then Gauss-Hermite integration over fresh difficulties) give the interval. Expected result: lower mean interval score than the naive Wilson baseline (combined_score -0.232), with the biggest gain at large K, small N and the halving searcher. Failure mode: real MMLU-Pro outcomes are multi-dimensional (subject x config interactions), so the 1-D model can be miscalibrated there. A held-out-question calibration widening factor and a bootstrap-style fallback are the safeguards.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"In program_1/src/program.py, keep the handle(request) python_rpc_v1 interface. reset stores the seed and returns exactly {'ok': True}. report returns exactly estimate, lower and upper, clipped so that 0<=lower<=estimate<=upper<=1 and all values are finite floats.\",\n    \"Decode log.question_idx, log.config_idx, log.outcome and subject_code from base64 little-endian arrays with np.frombuffer (dtypes uint16/uint8/uint8/uint8). Work only with arrays from the request, using no files or network.\",\n    \"Implement _fit_irt(q, c, y, subj, N, K): MAP fit of logit P = a[c] - b[q] by alternating damped Newton steps. Ridge priors a ~ N(0,tau^2), b ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), with a per-subject mean shrunk to the global mean. Update tau^2 and s^2 each outer iteration from the posterior second moments. Include the Laplace/trace correction so tau^2 does not collapse or inflate. Clamp tau^2 to [1e-4, 4].\",\n    \"Work only with sparse triples so the same code handles the exhaustive (N*K entries) and halving (sparse log) searchers. Use vectorised numpy with np.bincount for the per-config and per-question gradient and Hessian diagonals. Cap total outer iterations (about 30) and add a wall-clock guard (about 1.5 s) that returns the best current fit.\",\n    \"Posterior for the selected config: approximate Var(a_k) by the inverse of the diagonal Hessian plus the uncertainty in mu. Draw S (about 400) samples of (a_k, mu_global, log s) with the reset-seeded np.random.default_rng. For each draw compute theta = sum_j w_j * sigmoid(a_k - mu_sub_mix - s*x_j) using np.polynomial.hermite_e.hermegauss nodes, mixing over the empirical subject distribution when subject_code is not all zero.\",\n    \"Report estimate = posterior median or mean of theta, lower = np.quantile(theta, 0.05) and upper = np.quantile(theta, 0.95). Then apply a width-calibration factor kappa >= 1, applied around the estimate, that depends on min(N, evidence), K and whether subject_code is all zero (real vs synthetic).\",\n    \"Degenerate guards. If all outcomes are 0, return estimate 0, lower 0 and an upper bound no greater than a rule-of-three bound (about 3/n_selected_obs, capped, e.g. <=0.02). If the selected config has no observations, fall back to the pooled mean with a wide interval. If tau is estimated at its lower clamp (all configs equal), report a pooled-mean interval.\",\n    \"Build a local dev harness OUTSIDE program_1/src (for example dev/sim.py). It should generate synthetic latent-difficulty worlds across N in {50..2000} and K in {2..64}, plus equal-config worlds, and run both exhaustive and successive-halving searchers. It should also replay ../../task/resources/search/outcomes.npz (24 configs) with both searchers on subsampled question sets, compute the true y from held-out questions, and compute the mean interval score IS=(u-l)+20*max(l-y,0)+20*max(y-u,0). Compare the naive Wilson baseline, the posterior interval without calibration, and the calibrated version.\",\n    \"Use the dev harness to choose kappa and the clamps on the search split only. Check coverage near 0.90 in each (searcher, K, N) stratum. Check that the average per-call time is <=1 s and the worst-case is <=5 s on 4 threads. Set OMP/MKL threads to <=4. Optionally add a held-out-question calibration check inside report (a fit on 80% of questions, scoring the selected config on the other 20%) only if it improves IS in the dev harness.\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_agent\": \"Interface (from the baseline docstring in task/program_0/src/program.py): python_rpc_v1 handle(request). The ops are reset and report, with exact response key sets. numpy and scipy only, 4 threads, average <=2.7 s per call. The score is the interval score at 0.90, so the optimum is the 5%/95% predictive quantiles of y (the selected config's true fresh accuracy). A well-calibrated posterior interval beats a narrow Wilson interval because the 20x miss penalty is heavy at the winner's-curse bias. Configuration indices are relabelled at random per request, so use no index priors. Request mix is unlabelled: synthetic latent-difficulty worlds, equal-config worlds, all-zero worlds, and real MMLU-Pro (24 configs; exhaustive or successive halving). Use ../../task/resources/search (manifest.json, outcomes.npz) only to build the local dev harness. Do not read evaluate.py/evaluate_final.py to learn hidden details. Keep the implementation in the order of the plan. A secondary option, if the 1-D IRT is miscalibrated on real data: for the exhaustive searcher only, the question-bootstrap optimism estimate (resample questions, re-select the argmax, measure boot-selected in-bag mean minus its original-data mean) combined with the posterior interval, picking the wider of the two. Suggested code names for the rubric snippets: _fit_irt, tau2, kappa, hermegauss, np.quantile, time.perf_counter. If halving-round structure matters, it is inferable by grouping the log by question_idx (each question is evaluated by all survivors of its round). Since the likelihood is ignorable under adaptive selection, no round structure is needed for the base method. Limitations to record: 1-D latent difficulty vs real multi-dimensional subject x config effects; the estimated tau is noisy at small N.\",\n  \"target_rubrics\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"combined_score > -0.2320671337621132\",\n      \"id\": \"incumbent_score_improvement\",\n      \"metric\": \"combined_score\",\n      \"operator\": \">\",\n      \"threshold\": -0.2320671337621132,\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"program.py fits a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model a[config]-b[question] on the sparse (question, config, outcome) triples. It uses empirical-Bayes updates of the config-ability variance tau^2 and the difficulty variance s^2, via bincount-based vectorised Newton steps. The same code path serves both the exhaustive and the halving searchers, and the selected config's posterior is used for the report.\",\n      \"id\": \"hierarchical_irt_fit_on_sparse_log\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"def _fit_irt(\",\n          \"np.bincount(\",\n          \"tau2\",\n          \"hermegauss\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"The reported interval is the 5%/95% quantile of posterior draws of the selected config's fresh-question accuracy (Gauss-Hermite integration over fresh difficulties). A width-calibration factor kappa and ordering/clipping guarantees ensure 0<=lower<=estimate<=upper<=1, and the response has exactly the keys estimate, lower and upper.\",\n      \"id\": \"posterior_quantile_interval_with_calibration\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"np.quantile(\",\n          \"0.05\",\n          \"0.95\",\n          \"kappa\",\n          \"default_rng(\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"program.py handles all-zero outcomes (rule-of-three-style capped upper bound), equal-config worlds (tau at lower clamp gives a pooled-mean interval) and an unobserved selected config. It also enforces a per-call wall-clock guard well under 10 s via time.perf_counter.\",\n      \"id\": \"degenerate_world_and_time_guards\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\",\n      \"verification\": {\n        \"patterns\": [\n          \"time.perf_counter(\",\n          \"def handle(\"\n        ],\n        \"type\": \"source_contains\"\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"title\": \"Empirical-Bayes latent-difficulty posterior interval\"\n}","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace/paper","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
