{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwJS9C6PDCX1q66M6aMJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RqRymjfLsyVUkQLrzjQ134","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nS={\n\"status\":\"ready\",\n\"selected_prompt_family\":\"paper_standalone\",\n\"recommended_title\":\"Reporting Scores After Prompt Search: A Selection-Aware Posterior Interval from a Hierarchical Latent-Difficulty Model\",\n\"central_question\":\"When a configuration is chosen on a finite benchmark sample and its search-time score is then reported, how large is the optimism of that score, which factors control it, and can a selection-aware interval built only from the search-time evaluation log give a more reliable report of fresh-question accuracy?\",\n\"central_thesis\":\"The naive best-of-search score with a Wilson interval is biased upward and too narrow when many configurations are compared on few questions; an empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty posterior interval, fitted to the full logged (question, config, outcome) triples and agnostic to the searcher, lowers the mean 90%-level interval score relative to naive, Bonferroni-Wilson, and question-resampling reporters in the sealed final evaluation (qualified outcome), but it keeps residual positive bias and sub-nominal coverage in specific real-data regimes (successive halving, large N), so the claim is conditional on the studied worlds.\",\n\"narrative_rationale\":\"The final evaluation completed with outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome), mean interval score 0.1449 vs 0.2202 (naive), 0.1974 (Bonferroni), 0.2461 (resample) (final.metric.*), coverage 0.929 and null-control coverage 0.929. This gives a positive but narrow, conditional story. The task asks about the gap between reported and fresh-question accuracy and a more reliable reporting method; the run answers both through evaluator-owned synthetic worlds with exact truth and cached real Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct outcomes on MMLU-Pro under 24 prompt configurations, with exhaustive and successive-halving searchers and null/constant controls. A thesis-first estimator-and-evidence paper beats a campaign-chronology paper: the three model variants evaluated in search are close in score and only the frozen winner has a final evaluation, so they are best presented as a width/coverage trade-off comparison, not as a progression. Per-case results show where the method wins big (synthetic and real exhaustive, small N), where it is merely on par with naive (real halving, large N), and where a simple question-resampling reporter is slightly better (real exhaustive cases) or catastrophically bad (halving) - that heterogeneity is the scientifically interesting content and must be foregrounded for honesty. No downstream_study block exists in paper_data.json, so no downstream-study claims.\",\n\"prior_paper_story_status\":\"rejected\",\n\"edit_framing_decision\":\"Edit mode is not enabled (no previous_paper or edit context staged); there is no prior manuscript to preserve, broaden or recenter, so the story is chosen fresh from grounded results.\",\n\"staged_input_impact\":[\"No staged prior paper or new-program inputs exist; the framing rests only on paper_data.json, task contract, and the winning program source in the investigation directory.\"],\n\"method_groups\":[\n {\"name\":\"Naive best-of-search score with Wilson 90% interval\",\"role\":\"baseline\",\"include_reason\":\"The status-quo reporting practice the task questions; its signed bias (final.metric.naive_signed_bias = 0.0366) quantifies the optimism gap and the incumbent for scoring.\"},\n {\"name\":\"Bonferroni-widened Wilson interval and question-resampling (bootstrap-optimism) reporter\",\"role\":\"baseline\",\"include_reason\":\"Two evaluator-supplied selection-aware alternatives (final.metric.bonferroni_baseline_score, final.metric.resample_baseline_score); needed so the proposed gain is not against a strawman. Resample is better on some real exhaustive cases and fails under halving.\"},\n {\"name\":\"Empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty posterior interval (frozen selection)\",\"role\":\"proposed\",\"include_reason\":\"Winner of search (result.best_correct_score = -0.1480) and the evaluated method in the sealed final run. Logit P = a_config - b_question with EB variance estimates, ignorable-selection argument, Laplace draws plus Gauss-Hermite over fresh difficulties, 5/95% quantiles, calibration factor kappa, degenerate-world guards.\"},\n {\"name\":\"Search-stage variants: parametric-bootstrap pivot calibration; config-by-subject interaction random effects\",\"role\":\"ablation\",\"include_reason\":\"Two scored alternatives to the winner (result.top_program_02, result.top_program_03; 3 of 3 attempts scored, 0 unscored). Shows narrower intervals (c02 width 0.085, coverage 0.77) lose on interval score and extra interaction structure (c03) did not beat the plain model. Search-plane only; report as variant comparison, not as final evidence, and not as a significance ranking (single evaluation each).\"},\n {\"name\":\"Negative controls (synthetic null N1, real permuted-config null N2, constant-zero world Z1)\",\"role\":\"supporting\",\"include_reason\":\"Check the reporter does not manufacture signal and handles degenerate worlds (final.metric.null_control_abs_bias, null_control_coverage, constant_control_error = 0.0).\"}\n],\n\"results_focus\":[\n \"Sealed-final aggregate: mean interval score of the proposed reporter vs naive, Bonferroni and resample reporters (final.metric.*), with spread across the 8 final seeds (final.evidence.episodes). State outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome) with the completed status and 100% replication completion; do not extend into generality.\",\n \"Optimism gap and its drivers: naive signed bias per case (large at small N=60 ~0.051, smaller at N=200 ~0.022-0.025, ~0.009 at N=600; synthetic ~0.05-0.07; derived from per-case final episodes) versus the proposed reporter's residual bias; discuss N, K, searcher. Compute exact values from final.evidence.episodes; flag that per-case diagnostics have candidate_diagnostics_trust 'unverified'.\",\n \"Per-case interval score and coverage (synthetic P1,P2; real exhaustive P3,P5,P6; real halving P4) vs 0.90 nominal: large gains over naive on synthetic and small-N real cases, parity with naive on P4/P6, and sub-nominal coverage on P4 and P6; resample baseline slightly better on some real exhaustive cases and far worse under halving (P2, P4).\",\n \"Negative controls: null-control coverage 0.929 and absolute bias 0.017 vs naive; constant-zero error 0.0.\",\n \"Search-stage width-versus-coverage trade-off among three scored model variants (result.top_program_01..03; coverage 0.915 / 0.8925 / 0.77; widths 0.124 / 0.120 / 0.085), kept clearly separate from the final plane.\",\n \"Does the optimism gap change conclusions? Only supported to the extent that a miscalibrated interval's miss penalty and coverage show reported numbers are unreliable; do not claim ranking-reversal results unless computed from artifacts (none are in paper_data.json).\"\n],\n\"figure_plan\":[\n \"Fig 1 (overview, page 1-2): conceptual schematic of the selection-aware reporting problem: finite benchmark sample -> search over K configs -> selected config + evaluation log -> reporters (naive Wilson / Bonferroni / resampling / EB-IRT posterior) -> interval for fresh-question accuracy; shows the winner's-curse bias.\",\n \"Fig 2: per-case interval score (lower better) for four reporters, two panels (synthetic worlds incl. N1; real-LLM worlds incl. N2), grouped bars with seed-to-seed error bars.\",\n \"Fig 3: two-panel diagnostic: (a) signed bias of naive vs proposed reporter per case; (b) empirical 90% interval coverage per case with nominal line.\",\n \"Table 1: sealed-final aggregate metrics (mean interval score, coverage, width, bias, RMSE, controls) for the reporters. Table 2: search-stage variants (score, coverage, width, bias, null control).\",\n \"Optional Fig 4 (only if space): interval width versus N for the real exhaustive cases (P5 N=60, P3 N=200, P6 N=600) vs naive width, showing adaptation of width to evidence.\"\n],\n\"figure_specs\":[\n {\"figure_id\":\"overview_schematic\",\"required\":True,\"purpose\":\"Glance-value overview of the reporting problem and the reporters compared\",\"figure_type\":\"diagram\",\"preferred_generator\":\"generate-concept-figure\",\"group_by\":\"reporter\",\"metric\":\"none\",\"split_by\":\"none\",\"preferred_span\":\"full_width\",\"target_width_fraction\":1.0,\"layout_rationale\":\"Template is single-column with 5.5 in text width (paper.sty textwidth 5.5in); a left-to-right pipeline with 4 reporter boxes needs the full text width for legible 8-9 pt labels; place on page 1-2.\",\"allow_progression\":False,\"max_panels\":1,\"max_categories_per_panel\":6,\"max_series_per_panel\":4},\n {\"figure_id\":\"per_case_interval_score\",\"required\":True,\"purpose\":\"Compare mean interval score of naive, Bonferroni, resample and EB-IRT reporters per evaluation case, separated by synthetic vs real-LLM worlds\",\"figure_type\":\"grouped_bar\",\"preferred_generator\":\"matplotlib\",\"group_by\":\"reporter\",\"metric\":\"mean_interval_score\",\"split_by\":\"world_family\",\"preferred_span\":\"full_width\",\"target_width_fraction\":1.0,\"layout_rationale\":\"Two panels side by side at full 5.5 in width with 4 series and up to 5 cases per panel; column-style shrinking is unnecessary in a single-column template but halving the width would crowd labels. Export PDF + 300-DPI PNG; reference PDF in LaTeX. Clip/annotate the resample outlier on P2 (~0.59) with a broken-axis or labelled arrow rather than letting it crush the scale.\",\"allow_progression\":False,\"max_panels\":2,\"max_categories_per_panel\":5,\"max_series_per_panel\":4},\n {\"figure_id\":\"bias_and_coverage\",\"required\":True,\"purpose\":\"Show optimism gap (signed bias) and interval coverage per case, naive vs proposed, against 0 and nominal 0.90\",\"figure_type\":\"paired_dot_or_bar\",\"preferred_generator\":\"matplotlib\",\"group_by\":\"reporter\",\"metric\":\"signed_bias_and_coverage\",\"split_by\":\"diagnostic\",\"preferred_span\":\"full_width\",\"target_width_fraction\":1.0,\"layout_rationale\":\"Two panels (bias, coverage) over 8 cases; full text width keeps case labels (P1..P6,N1,N2) readable; 2 series per panel.\",\"allow_progression\":False,\"max_panels\":2,\"max_categories_per_panel\":6,\"max_series_per_panel\":2},\n {\"figure_id\":\"width_vs_n\",\"required\":False,\"purpose\":\"Show interval width adapting to N in real exhaustive cases (P5,P3,P6) vs naive width\",\"figure_type\":\"line_or_bar\",\"preferred_generator\":\"matplotlib\",\"group_by\":\"reporter\",\"metric\":\"mean_width\",\"split_by\":\"none\",\"preferred_span\":\"full_width\",\"target_width_fraction\":0.7,\"layout_rationale\":\"Single panel with 3 N values, can be placed at ~0.7 text width; include only if body-page budget requires added substance and the values come from final episodes; otherwise move to supplement.\",\"allow_progression\":False,\"max_panels\":1,\"max_categories_per_panel\":3,\"max_series_per_panel\":2}\n],\n\"supplementary_plan\":{\n \"purpose\":\"Provide reimplementation-level detail and full per-case/per-seed tables that support but do not carry the central claim, plus search-stage variant details.\",\n \"planned_subsections\":[\n  {\"heading\":\"Estimator details and hyperparameters\",\"grounded_content\":\"Full specification of the EB-IRT reporter: logistic model, priors, EM-style variance updates, Laplace draws (400), Gauss-Hermite nodes (21), kappa calibration, degenerate-world guards (all-zero upper cap 0.02), wall-clock guard (1.5 s fit budget). Taken from the program source of the frozen winner.\",\"source_ids\":[\"result.best_correct_score\",\"result.top_program_01\"]},\n  {\"heading\":\"Per-case and per-seed final results\",\"grounded_content\":\"Full table of interval score, coverage, width, bias for each case P1-P6, N1, N2, Z1 and each of the eight final seeds, for all four reporters, derived from the final episode records.\",\"source_ids\":[\"final.evidence.episodes\",\"final.metric.combined_score\",\"final.metric.coverage\"]},\n  {\"heading\":\"Search-stage model variants\",\"grounded_content\":\"Metrics of the three scored variants (EB-IRT, parametric-bootstrap pivot, config-by-subject interaction): score, coverage, width, bias, RMSE, null-control coverage, baselines; 3 of 3 attempts scored, none unscored.\",\"source_ids\":[\"result.top_program_01\",\"result.top_program_02\",\"result.top_program_03\",\"result.total_attempts\",\"result.total_evaluated_programs\"]},\n  {\"heading\":\"Evaluation protocol and data provenance\",\"grounded_content\":\"Worlds, searchers, K/N/R per case, interval-score definition (level 0.90, worst-case 20), aggregation weights (0.4 synthetic/0.6 real), dataset and model revisions (MMLU-Pro rev b189ec76..., Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct rev 7ae55760...), software versions, seeds 9101-9108, compute reported as measured (episode durations ~6 s each).\",\"source_ids\":[\"final.evidence.episodes\",\"final.qualification.outcome\",\"final.qualification.status\"]},\n  {\"heading\":\"LLM usage disclosure\",\"grounded_content\":\"Required ICLR LLM Usage statement describing the role of LLM in ideation, implementation and writing for this study.\",\"source_ids\":[\"result.total_ideas\"]}\n ]\n},\n\"forbidden_headings\":[\"Search Campaign Chronology\",\"First Successful Candidate\",\"Milestones\",\"Progression of Variants\",\"Iteration 1/2/3\",\"Experiment A / Experiment B\",\"What We Tried\",\"Lessons from the Run\"],\n\"forbidden_phrases\":[\"correctness rate\",\"correct and eligible\",\"eligible\",\"combined_score\",\"rubric\",\"worker_01\",\"state of the art\",\"we discovered that\",\"after several iterations\",\"first valid\",\"incumbent\",\"program_1\",\"idea_id\",\"provably valid\",\"guarantees coverage\",\"outperforms in all settings\",\"generalizes to all LLMs\"],\n\"notes_for_writer\":[\n \"Final outcome is 'qualified' with status 'completed' (final.qualification.outcome/status/correct); state it plainly, but publication eligibility does not equal broad scientific validity: keep claims conditional (one 0.5B model, MMLU-Pro, 24 prompt configs, two searchers: exhaustive and successive halving, evaluator-owned synthetic worlds; no DSPy/MIPRO, no adaptive Ladder-style reporting).\",\n \"Present the metric as 'mean interval score at 90% level (lower is better)'; the internal combined_score is its negative and must not appear by name. Interval score = width + 20x miss distance; worst-case failed replication IS=20.\",\n \"No downstream_study block is present: do not describe a downstream study. Keep search-plane (three variants) separate from final-plane (frozen winner only). Per-variant final results do not exist; do not invent them. Search score differences among variants (-0.1480, -0.1519, -0.1562) come from one scored evaluation each and are not a significance ranking.\",\n \"The final per-case diagnostics are derived from final.evidence.episodes with candidate_diagnostics_trust='unverified'; aggregate metrics in final.metric.* are the authoritative numbers. Compute per-case means and across-seed spread from episode records with a script included in the code package; uncertainty = spread over the 8 final seeds (9101-9108), each with R=30 replications (R=10 for Z1).\",\n \"Be honest about heterogeneity: proposed reporter is roughly on par with naive on real halving (P4: 0.156 vs 0.161, with larger signed bias 0.031 vs 0.025) and at large N (P6: 0.096 vs 0.096), with coverage below 0.90 on P4 (~0.89) and P6 (~0.83); resample baseline is slightly better on several real exhaustive cases (P3, P5, P6, N2) but very poor under halving (P2 ~0.59, P4 ~0.26). Recompute these from episodes before quoting.\",\n \"Frame the answer to research question 1 via the naive signed bias (0.0366 aggregate) and its dependence on N, K, world, and searcher; do not claim a decision-reversal effect without a computed artifact. Treat 'does it affect conclusions' as partially addressed through coverage/miss penalty and say what is not tested.\",\n \"The real-LLM outcome tensor (Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct on MMLU-Pro, 24 configs, pinned revisions) was shipped with the task package and derived by the task authors; this run did not regenerate it. Say so. Do not claim the paper ran its own LLM inference or its own DSPy/MIPRO searches. Report actual compute: final evaluation episodes ~6 s each (from episode duration fields).\",\n \"Mechanism motivation: selection by searchers uses only observed outcomes, so selection is ignorable for the likelihood, which licenses one code path for both searchers; shrinkage of config effects removes winner's-curse bias; the 5/95% quantiles minimise expected interval score for the predictive distribution. State the ignorability argument as motivation, not as proven in the real-data regime, since 1-D difficulty is misspecified for real multi-dimensional subject-by-config effects (c03 attempted that extension in search and did not beat the plain model).\",\n \"Related work: use only references in references.bib that are real and relevant (Dwork et al. 2015 adaptive-data-analysis entries; reusable holdout; Feldman/Steinke-style variance work; the Ullman et al. perspective). The bib contains many irrelevant medical/unrelated entries; do not cite them. Starter-literature works (MMLU-Pro, DSPy, MIPRO, Ladder, Inference on Winners, Valid post-selection inference, Sclar et al., Polo et al.) are not in references.bib; cite them only after verified retrieval/addition, otherwise describe the relation without a citation.\",\n \"Overview figure uses generate-concept-figure (only one concept figure planned). All figures full text width (5.5 in single-column ICLR template); export PDF and 300-DPI PNG; reference the PDF in LaTeX; never upscale rasters.\",\n \"Body budget 8-9 pages: spend space on problem formalization (selection, truth definition, interval score), the estimator with enough detail to reimplement, the per-case comparison, bias/coverage diagnostics, null controls, and a substantial limitations section (single small LLM, two searchers, evaluator-defined worlds, kappa calibrated on a local development harness using the search split, residual undercoverage, no adaptive reporting). Do not pad with search narrative.\",\n \"Anonymize: no author names, no self-identifying links or run directory paths; include LLM Usage section (appendix permitted).\",\n \"Threshold-style rates: if one is used, use 'interval coverage' (fraction of intervals containing the truth) with a single meaning; do not introduce other thresholded labels.\"\n],\n\"failure_reason\":\"\"\n}\njson.dump(S,open('paper_strategy.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nE\npython3 -c \"import json;json.load(open('/workspace/paper/paper_strategy.json'));print('ok')\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3683,"cache_read_input_tokens":66310,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3683},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d9f3fff-e236-4c90-ae14-7a47dddbd380","uuid":"8a6cf45a-9c20-425a-bde5-4abea7b60255","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:31:24.012Z","request_id":"req_011CgwJS8YQSv1cXe7JrYh9d"}
